<%#- The ONE component with a per-variant template. DaisyUI expresses a page
     item as two tokens (`join-item btn`), so each of the six variants below can
     repeat them inline — which is exactly what plain Tailwind cannot do. -%>
<%%# locals: (page: 1, page_count: 1) -%>
<%%# The numbered page control. It lives INSIDE the replaced fragment, like
    every other control in there: page_count is a server number, so a
    single-page list simply stops rendering this element. Placed outside the
    fragment it would never be re-rendered and could only ever change its TEXT
    (reactive values are textContent-only), leaving a control whose links point
    at pages that no longer exist.

    SCROLL. The links are anchors to the list, not buttons, and that IS the
    scroll answer — the client only preventDefaults `submit`, so the click both
    performs the action and does the browser's own fragment jump, which puts the
    top of the list back in view exactly as a full page load would. No client
    capability needed. data-turbo="false" keeps Turbo Drive out of it: a
    same-page hash with Turbo off is a plain scroll, not a navigation.

    No generation token — a page number is idempotent, so a double fire is one
    write and one render.

    `on` returns a { data: } hash, so an element needing its OWN data attributes
    has to MERGE rather than splat: a bare ** after `data: { turbo: false }`
    would silently replace it and the anchor would stop scrolling. %>
<%%# PENDING. Nothing here asks for it: the client stamps data-hibiki-busy on
    the control that fired, so the clicked page link dims itself while the trip
    is out, and the swap that answers it destroys the attribute along with the
    link. The bar above the list is the other half — the anchor jump has
    already put it in view by then. %>
<%% page_data = ->(n) { { turbo: false }.merge(on(:go_to_page, with: { page: n })[:data]) } %>
<%%# The id-carrying wrapper stays put and stays BARE. It always renders, so
    idiomorph always has a node to pair with, and it holds no layout classes of
    its own — with no page control it is empty, and padding here would leave
    dead vertical space on a list that has none. %>
<div id="<%= pagination_dom_id %>">
  <%% if page_count > 1 %>
    <nav<%= css_attr(:pagination_nav) %> aria-label="Pagination">
      <%%# Prev/next stay rendered and go inert at the ends rather than
          disappearing, so the control keeps its width across a page change —
          one that reflows under the pointer is how you mis-click a list that
          just repainted. A span, not a disabled link: there is no page to
          name, so there is no action to stamp. %>
      <%% if page == 1 %>
        <span class="join-item btn btn-disabled" aria-disabled="true">«</span>
      <%% else %>
        <%%= link_to "«", "#<%= list_dom_id %>", class: "join-item btn", rel: "prev",
                    "aria-label": "Previous page", data: page_data.(page - 1) %>
      <%% end %>

      <%% <%= query_class_name %>.page_numbers(page: page, page_count: page_count).each do |n| %>
        <%% if n.nil? %>
          <span class="join-item btn btn-disabled" aria-hidden="true">…</span>
        <%% elsif n == page %>
          <%%# aria-current is the only thing that tells a screen reader which
              page it is on: the visual cue is a class, and the link text is
              just a number either way. %>
          <span class="join-item btn btn-active" aria-current="page"><%%= n %></span>
        <%% else %>
          <%%= link_to n, "#<%= list_dom_id %>", class: "join-item btn",
                      "aria-label": "Page #{n}", data: page_data.(n) %>
        <%% end %>
      <%% end %>

      <%% if page == page_count %>
        <span class="join-item btn btn-disabled" aria-disabled="true">»</span>
      <%% else %>
        <%%= link_to "»", "#<%= list_dom_id %>", class: "join-item btn", rel: "next",
                    "aria-label": "Next page", data: page_data.(page + 1) %>
      <%% end %>
    </nav>
  <%% end %>
</div>
